prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages schedule overrides for zones and their actuators in a smart building automation platform.

The people involved are facility managers, zone controllers, maintenance technicians, and tenants, and one person may hold both the facility manager and zone controller roles at the same time.

The resources are actuators, zones, and commission requests: every actuator records its owner, zone group, device tags, maintenance flag, and status; every zone records its assigned controller, criticality level, and status; every commission request records the device involved, who requested it, and its status.

Each requester carries a control tier ranging from level 1 through level 4 and a list of the groups they belong to.

A zone controller or facility manager may override a zone's schedule only when their control tier dominates that zone's criticality level, so a control tier of 3 can override a zone rated critical while a control tier of 1 cannot.

A zone controller may override a zone's schedule only when they are that zone's assigned controller, and a zone controller who is assigned to a different zone must not override this zone's schedule.

A facility manager may override a zone's schedule only when they also currently hold the zone controller role, and a facility manager who does not hold the zone controller role must not override any zone's schedule.

A schedule may be overridden only when the zone's status is not locked, and when the zone's status is locked no override may take effect no matter how high the requester's control tier is.

Neither a zone controller nor a facility manager may override a zone's schedule unless the tier, assignment, role, and lock requirements above are all satisfied, and a maintenance technician or tenant must never override a zone's schedule under any circumstances.

A request by a zone controller who is that zone's assigned controller, whose control tier dominates the zone's criticality level, and whose zone is not locked, to override that zone's schedule must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity User {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    controlTier: Long,
    groups: Set<Group>,
};

entity Role enum ["facilityManager", "zoneController", "maintenanceTechnician", "tenant"];

entity Group;

entity Actuator {
    owner: User,
    zoneGroup: Group,
    deviceTags: Set<String>,
    maintenanceFlag: Bool,
    status: String,
};

entity Zone {
    assignedController: User,
    criticalityLevel: Long,
    status: String,
};

entity CommissionRequest {
    device: Actuator,
    requestedBy: User,
    status: String,
};

action overrideZoneSchedule appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Zone],
};
"
}
